  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 125 PM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nThe dry weather pattern will continue through at least Thursday as a \ndry air mass remains in place. The aforementioned high pressure \nsystem will work its way over the northeastern CONUS Tuesday night. \nBy Wednesday, the upper-level flow will become more northwesterly \nunder broad troughing over southeastern Canada. Southerly return \nflow will continue in our area, resulting in high temperatures \nrising to the middle to upper 70s for Wednesday. The main focus for \nadjustments for this forecast continues to be lowering the forecast \ndew points closer to the 10th percentile of the model spectrum \nthrough Wednesday, considering the consensus blends tend to \nunderperform on the PBL mixing in dry air masses. Ultimately, this \nwill result in lower relative humidity.\n\nOur lone chance of any precipitation continues to be for Thursday \nnight, when a weak shortwave in the mid-levels is progged to \napproach the region. Mesoscale forcing will be enhanced due to a \nwarm frontal zone draped over Iowa. With the drier air in place, \nchances of measurable rainfall remain low, with both the NBM and \nLREF probabilities of measurable rain only around 10-30%, mainly \nover southeastern Iowa.\n\nBy next weekend, ridging at the surface and aloft is forecast to \nbuild into the region ahead of a 500 mb trough in the Southern \nPlains. At this time, it looks like warm and dry weather will \nprevail through next weekend. Temperatures on Saturday will be 5 to \n10 degrees above normal with high temperatures both days in the 70s \nand even some isolated lower 80s. Both days will also be breezy with \nspeeds of 10 to 15 MPH and gusts of 20 to 25 MPH.",
